```cpp
#include <chrono>
#include <iostream>

void update_book();

int main() {
    using clk = std::chrono::steady_clock;
    auto t0 = clk::now();
    update_book();
    auto t1 = clk::now();
    auto ns = std::chrono::duration_cast<std::chrono::nanoseconds>(t1 - t0).count();
    std::cout << ns << " ns\n";
}
```

I use `steady_clock` because it is monotonic: it never jumps backwards and is not affected by NTP or someone changing the wall clock. `system_clock` can jump, and `high_resolution_clock` is just an alias of `system_clock` in libstdc++. For very short functions, time a batch of many calls and divide, because `now()` itself costs ~20 ns.

```cpp
int max_branchless(int a, int b) {
    return a ^ ((a ^ b) & -(a < b));   // -(a<b) is all ones if a<b, else 0
}
// or simply: return (a > b) ? a : b;  // -O2 on x86-64 usually emits cmp + cmov
```

A branch costs nothing when predicted correctly, but a misprediction flushes the pipeline (~15-20 cycles). Branchless code (cmov or mask arithmetic) has no branch to mispredict, so it wins when the condition is unpredictable, e.g. random market data where each side is taken ~50 % of the time.

It is not faster when the branch is highly predictable (e.g. an error check that is almost never true) or when one side is expensive: branchless code always computes both sides and adds a data dependency, so the branchy version can be equal or faster. Measure.

```cpp
#include <array>
#include <cstdint>

constexpr std::array<std::int64_t, 19> make_pow10() {
    std::array<std::int64_t, 19> t{};
    std::int64_t v = 1;
    for (auto& x : t) { x = v; v *= 10; }
    return t;
}
constexpr auto POW10 = make_pow10();
static_assert(POW10[3] == 1000);

inline std::int64_t to_ticks(std::int64_t px, int dec) { return px * POW10[dec]; }
```

The table is computed at compile time and stored in `.rodata`, so there is no runtime initialisation. At runtime each use is a single load from a tiny table that stays in L1 (~1 ns) plus a multiply, instead of calling `std::pow` (tens of ns, floating point, rounding issues) or a loop. `static_assert` also checks the values at compile time.
